## Flow

### Setup

1. Capture the request. Words naming an existing `request.md` — alone or
   with other words{% if followup %}, such as the prior working dir, remote
   or base below{% endif %} — → its directory is the working dir. Otherwise,
   words that are one path and nothing else — to a file not named
   `request.md`, or to a `request.md` that does not exist — are not a
   request to transcribe: STOP — one line naming the path and the required
   name `request.md` — end turn. Any other words are a chat request → pick
   the working dir (per the project's
   documentation standard if any){% if stack %}, outside the repo — a
   standard that places it inside → ask the user for one, end turn{% endif %};
   write the request there verbatim, with this one line above it:

   > *Provenance: the user's own words, transcribed from chat by the orchestrator.*

{% if stack %}
   The **request slug** is the working dir's basename (e.g.
   `12-fix-login-timeout`).
{% if followup %}
   The user's words must also name the **prior working dir**, the shipped
   cycle's working dir — the directory holding its `design.md`, whatever
   its request file is named; absent → ask once, end turn. A path given
   as the prior working dir, the directory or any file in it, is never the
   request.
   Words pointing at an existing `request.md` may also name the
   **remote**, the git remote holding the stack's PRs: recorded when they
   do, never asked for. A request written from chat records none — the
   liaison settles the remote from the request itself.
   The same words may also name the **base**, a local branch to re-create
   the whole stack on, in any plain words ("onto `<branch>`", "re-create
   the stack on `<branch>`", "rebase onto …"): recorded as the name and
   `git rev-parse refs/heads/<name>`; a name that does not resolve locally →
   STOP, say so, end turn. Read from the user's words in either shape of
   request, never asked for; absent → a plain follow-up.
{% endif %}
2. A rebase or `git am` is in progress — `git rev-parse --git-path
   rebase-merge` or `--git-path rebase-apply` names an existing directory
   — → STOP, say so, end turn: it is the user's to finish or abort.{% if followup %}
   `git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no` prints anything → STOP,
   say tracked files have uncommitted changes, end turn: the user commits
   or stashes them before launching. Record HEAD (`git rev-parse HEAD`). A detached HEAD is no stop: the work branch
   is created from the top stack branch, or the base, either way.{% else %} Record
   the original base (`git rev-parse HEAD`). A detached HEAD is no
   stop: the work branch is created from HEAD either way.{% endif +%}
{% else %}
2. In every repository of the scope — the primary, and each the launch
   words add (**Scope phrase**, below) — record the original base and the
   branch. Already detached at setup → STOP, ask the user which branch to
   work on, end turn.
{% endif %}
3. Record the repo, the working dir and the artifact repo (`git -C <working
   dir> rev-parse --show-toplevel`). A working dir under no git repository →
   STOP, say so, end turn: the freeze needs a commit to compare against.{% if stack %}
   The working dir is inside the repo — its `realpath` lies under `git
   rev-parse --show-toplevel` run in the repo, which also covers a
   submodule of it — → STOP, say so and why, end turn: a stack
   workflow keeps its artifacts in a sidecar repository, because the
   pr-assembler rewrites the repo's history and the log and the PR records
   must stay outside what it rewrites.
{% if followup %}
4. **Fetch** (**PR liaison**). Then `git rev-parse HEAD` must equal the
   recorded HEAD and `git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no` print
   nothing; otherwise → STOP, surface both states, end turn,
   `status=ESCALATE`: the user puts the repository back. `ESCALATE` → STOP, surface the exception
   path, end turn, `status=ESCALATE`; no `stack=` or `stack=-` → the same,
   surfacing the target.
5. **Stack check** on `stack=<b1>,…,<bn>`: every `git rev-parse
   refs/heads/<b_i>` resolves; for `i` < `n`, `git rev-parse <b_{i+1}>^`
   equals `<b_i>`'s commit; `git rev-list --merges <b1>^..<bn>` is empty;
   `git rev-list --first-parent --count <b1>^..<bn>` is `n`. Any failure →
   STOP, quote the command and output, end turn, `status=ESCALATE`.
   Record **inherited** = `(b_i, c_i)` bottom first, the names and the
   commits the check resolved. No base → the **stack** = inherited, sizes
   and `pr-r` paths `-`, original base `<b1>^` and PR base `<bn>`. A base →
   the **stack** is empty, and original base = PR base = the base's commit.
6. **Work branch** `<request slug>-stack-work` at `<start>` — `refs/heads/<bn>`
   without a base, `refs/heads/<base>` with one: `git checkout --no-track -b
   <name> <start>`, so no `branch.autoSetupMerge` setting ties it to an
   upstream.
{% else %}
4. **Work branch** `<request slug>-stack-work`: `git checkout --no-track
   -b <name>`, so no `branch.autoSetupMerge` setting ties it to an upstream.
{% endif %}
   It fails — the name is not a valid branch name, or it or a ref it
   conflicts with already exists; git writes nothing then — → STOP, quote
   git's error and the name, say the user relaunches from a working dir
   whose basename is a valid branch name or deletes or renames the
   leftover branch, end turn. Never sanitize the name into another: the
   user finds the branch by the slug. You never delete a branch you did
   not create in this cycle.

   It succeeds → record it as **branch**{% if followup %}; HEAD = round base = review
   base = `<start>`'s commit{% endif %}. The branch checked out at launch{% if followup %},
   like the previous cycle's work branch,{% endif +%}
   is never recorded and never touched again; it stays where it was. This
   is the last setup step, so no setup stop leaves the branch behind.
{% endif %}

{% if stack %}
The stack chains run in one repository. User words with a scope phrase —
`in scope:`, `also in scope:` or `scope:` followed by a path — at setup or
at any stop → STOP, say the stack chains run in exactly one repository, end
turn.
{% else %}
**Scope phrase.** The scope is widened only by an explicit scope phrase in
the user's words: `in scope:`, `also in scope:` or `scope:`, followed by one
or more paths, at launch or at any stop. A path mentioned any other way —
for context, in a request, beside a file reference — never widens the
scope: widening grants write access to a tree another session may be
using, and the user's words make it a deliberate act. For each path: `git -C
<path> rev-parse --show-toplevel` must succeed and that toplevel's HEAD must
be attached, else STOP naming the path, end turn; the **toplevel** is what
is recorded, so a subdirectory names its repository. The primary, a
repository already recorded, and the artifact repo are refused by name. The
request's text never sets the scope — you read nothing — so a request that
says "we will change both repositories" is designed against the scope the
words set, and the design's **Scope** section raises the gap at the gate.

When the artifact repo is the repo — the working dir inside the primary — a
scope phrase at launch (checked at **Setup** step 3) or at any stop → STOP,
say that more than one repository in scope requires the ADR directory in a
separate repository (the sidecar layout), end turn. In that layout the
freeze and every artifact commit move the primary's HEAD, so the ship
sequence cannot run there.

At every stop after setup, when the scope holds more than the primary, the
surfaced lines include one line stating it back: `scope: <path> (<branch>),
<path> (<branch>)` — the user sees what the cycle may write to before
answering.
{% endif %}

### Design

1. `ccc run designer`, **draft** mode. + target design path{% if followup %}, + the
   comments path (the latest `pr-comments-<K>.md`){% endif %}.
2. Run the **Review chain** for phase `design` (reviewer `design-reviewer`,
   responder `designer`). Escalation → carry the verdict path to the gate.
{% if followup %}
   Every `design-reviewer` launch and its `designer` **respond** add every
   `pr-comments-*` path, never as notes.
{% endif %}

### Design gate{% if decide and fg_design %} — forester holds it{% endif %}

{% set forester_brief %}**Forester gate**, mode design, id `r<R>-g<G>`: + the document path, + this
review's notes, dispositions and verdict paths, + any escalation verdict{% if followup %}, + every
`pr-comments-*` and `pr-posted-*` path that exists{% endif %}{% endset %}
{% set rendering_launch %}`ccc run eli5-explainer`, mode `spec` for `design.md` → target
`design-eli5.md`, mode `delta` for a delta → target `design-eli5-delta-<N>.md`{% endset %}
{% if gate_posts %}
**Post** (**PR liaison**) first, on every entry to `design.md`'s gate; a
delta's gate never posts. **Post** runs on a gate entry the review chain
cleared; on an entry that carries an escalation verdict it runs on the
user's word to proceed. An entry carries one when a judge `ESCALATE` is
carried to it: from **Design** step 2, or from the `judge` after a return's
`designer` **respond**. **Post** `ESCALATE` → STOP, surface the exception
path, end turn, `status=ESCALATE`, untriaged; words to retry run **Post**
again.

{% endif %}
{% if eli5_at_gate %}
**Gate prep**, on every entry to this gate — the first after the design
review, every return after a feedback round, and each delta's gate (**Spec
deltas**). The document on the table is `design.md` before the freeze, or
`design-delta-<N>.md`. The rendering's target is the same path on every
entry: it is a draft like the document it renders, rewritten in place until
the gate clears it, and frozen with it. The gate is re-entered only after a
designer launch touched the document, so every entry renders. The rendering
is not reviewed.

{% if gate_pair %}
Two launches, neither reading the other's output: {{ forester_brief }};
and {{ rendering_launch }}. Both must have replied before you STOP.

{% if harness == "claude" %}
Issue both `ccc run` commands before waiting on either, each through the
shell tool's long-running mode exactly as **Spawning subagents through ccc**
says; a reply is read when its command finishes, and nothing in **Cut-off
launch** changes — each launch is recovered on its own, by its own agent
