### Assembly check

Run instead of **Attachment** and **Movement** for the assembler's launch,
which a rebase cut off or left unfinished leaves detached:

0. HEAD is attached to **branch** (`git symbolic-ref -q HEAD`) and no rebase
   is in progress: neither `git rev-parse --git-path rebase-merge` nor
   `--git-path rebase-apply` names an existing directory.
1. `git merge-base --is-ancestor <original base> HEAD`; `git rev-list
   --merges <original base>..HEAD` is empty; `git rev-list --first-parent
   --count <original base>..HEAD` is the stack's length — the recorded length
   plus one on `assembled`, unchanged on `restacked`.
2. `git diff --name-only <pre-assembly HEAD> HEAD` prints nothing, or only
   the path the reply's `changelog=` names. Any other path fails the check:
   the tree that was reviewed is the tree that ships.
3. `git rev-list --reverse --first-parent <original base>..HEAD` lists the
   stack's commits bottom first; the *i*-th recorded branch — and on
   `assembled` the reply's `branch=` as the last — resolves (`git rev-parse
   refs/heads/<b>`) to the *i*-th commit; the reply's `branch=` is not
   already in the stack.

All four hold → the assembly stands. Check 0 holds, HEAD unmoved, every stack
branch at its recorded commit,{% if followup %} every inherited branch not in
the stack at its setup commit,{% endif %} no local branch beyond the recorded names and
no tracked change (`git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no` prints
nothing) → nothing was assembled: an `ESCALATE` goes to
**Triage** as usual, and an `assembled` or `restacked` goes to **Triage**
with the log path as the triggering document, as a second `tree=dirty`
does. Anything else → **Repo anomaly** with only `restore` on the table,
which here is: `git rebase --abort` when check 0 found a rebase in progress;
`git checkout -f -B <branch> <pre-assembly HEAD>`, which also discards what
the launch staged or wrote to tracked files; `git branch -f <b_i> <recorded
c_i>` for every recorded stack branch;{% if followup %} `git branch -f <b> <setup commit>`
for every inherited branch not in the stack;{% endif %} `git branch -D` of every local branch
absent from the recorded names, each named when you state the repair. Every
pre-restack commit is reachable from the recorded refs, so nothing is lost. `adopt` is never
offered: a stack the check rejects is not one the record can describe.
After the restore, run this check again in place of **HEAD check** step 2;
it finds nothing assembled and routes the reply from there, or, with no
reply, returns to **Launch failure**.
{% else %}
